"""Extra chapter text. Numbers used here are explained again from metrics.json in the builder."""


def _split(text: str) -> list[str]:
    blocks = []
    for part in text.strip().split("\n\n"):
        line = " ".join(part.split())
        if line:
            blocks.append(line)
    return blocks


COLUMN_NOTES = {
    "Student_Age": "Question 1 asks the age group. The answers are 18-21, 22-25 and above 26. I did not use the code 1, 2, 3 as a number, because 3 is not three times 1. It is only a group name. A teacher may think older students study differently, but age alone cannot decide Excellent or Poor. I kept the column because it is in the published questionnaire and it is known before the final grade.",
    "Sex": "Question 2 is sex, female or male. I decoded it so that the data dictionary is complete, and then I removed it from the model. A study-help project should look at attendance, notes and study hours. Sex is not a step the student can take before the exam. Leaving it in the tree earlier made the model use it too much, so the deployed feature list does not contain it.",
    "High_School_Type": "Question 3 is the type of high school: private, state or other. This is background. The student cannot change the old school during the present semester. I still kept it because the UCI form includes it and some papers use school type as a background attribute. It is one-hot encoded, so private is not greater than state.",
    "Scholarship": "Question 4 is the scholarship level: none, 25 percent, 50 percent, 75 percent or full. In the decision-tree description, the 75 percent answer received a large split. That is a description of this small file, not a rule that a scholarship causes a grade. The final selected model is SVM, which does not print this split. I kept the column because scholarship is recorded before the end-of-term grade.",
    "Additional_Work": "Question 5 asks whether the student does additional work, yes or no. A job can reduce study time. It can also mean the student is managing time. The column is only yes or no, so it does not say how many hours the job takes. I kept it as a habit-related question. The tree description gave some weight to the No answer. I do not treat that weight as advice to quit a job.",
    "Sports_Activity": "Question 6 asks if the student does a regular sports activity. Sports can take time, and it can also be a routine. The file does not say which sport or how many days. I kept the yes/no answer. I did not invent a weekly sports hour column, because that number is not in the UCI file.",
    "Partner": "Question 7 asks whether the student has a partner. This is a personal question. I kept it only because it is one of the published columns and the model comparison uses the same questionnaire. I would not show this answer on a public rank list. In the website the prediction form still asks it, because dropping it only in the form and keeping it in training would make the form and the model different.",
    "Salary": "Question 8 is a salary band in US dollars, from 135-200 up to above 410. The file does not say whether this is the student's salary or the family salary. I treated it as a category. The band USD 271-340 appeared in the tree description. Income is not a study action. I have written in the results chapter that background columns should not be used to rank a student in public.",
    "Transportation": "Question 9 is how the student reaches college: bus, private car or taxi, bicycle, or other. Travel can affect attendance if the distance is long, but the question does not ask the distance. I kept the four answers as separate categories. Bicycle is not a higher value than bus.",
    "Accommodation": "Question 10 is where the student lives: rental, dormitory, with family, or other. Living place can change study time, but the column is only the type of stay. I did not add a distance or a rent amount. Those values are not in the file.",
    "Mother_Education": "Question 11 is the mother's education, from primary school to PhD. In the decision-tree description this column, especially the high-school answer, had the largest split among the deployed columns. I am not saying a mother's school decides the grade. On 145 rows a tree can split on a chance pattern. SVM is the selected model and it does not use that split as a printed rule. I kept the column because it is in the questionnaire, and I excluded it from any claim of cause.",
    "Father_Education": "Question 12 is the father's education, with the same levels as the mother question. The tree description also used the MSc answer. Education of parents is background. The student cannot change it before the internal exam. It stays in the feature list so that the experiment matches the published questions, except the columns I have named as excluded.",
    "Siblings": "Question 13 is the number of sisters and brothers, grouped as 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5 or above. I did not use it as a numeric count in a formula. Each group is its own category after one-hot encoding. A larger family is not coded as a higher mark.",
    "Parental_Status": "Question 14 is parental status: married, divorced, or one or both parents died. This is sensitive. The tree description used the third answer. I kept the column for the same reason as the other family questions, and the report says this kind of column must not be used as a public label for a student. The website is a local demo, not a college notice board.",
    "Mother_Occupation": "Question 15 is the mother's occupation: retired, housewife, government officer, private sector, self-employment or other. Occupation is not an ordered scale. A government officer is not greater than a private employee. One-hot encoding is the reason I converted the codes to words first.",
    "Father_Occupation": "Question 16 is the father's occupation. The list is retired, government officer, private sector, self-employment or other. There is no housewife code on this question in the UCI description I followed. I did not add one. Unknown codes stop the program instead of being mapped to a nearby job.",
    "Weekly_Study_Hours": "Question 17 is weekly study hours: none, less than 5, 6-10, 11-20, or more than 20. This is the kind of column a teacher expects in a performance project. I kept it. The groups are still categories. I did not convert them into the numbers 0, 5, 10 and 20, because the original answers are ranges, not exact hours. A student who studies 6 hours and a student who studies 10 hours are in the same group.",
    "Reading_Non_Scientific": "Question 18 asks about reading non-scientific books: none, sometimes or often. Reading can be a habit, but the question does not say which books. I kept the three answers. Often is not stored as the number 3 in the model.",
    "Reading_Scientific": "Question 19 is the same style of question for scientific books and journals. I kept it separate from question 18. A student can read one type often and the other type never. Merging them would hide that difference, so I left both columns in the deployed list.",
    "Seminar_Attendance": "Question 20 asks whether the student attends seminars, yes or no. The tree description gave a small weight to the No answer. Seminar attendance is an academic activity, so it fits the project topic better than salary. It is still only yes or no. The file has no seminar count.",
    "Project_Impact": "Question 21 asks the impact of projects on success: positive, negative or neutral. This is the student's own opinion. It is not the project mark. I kept it as an opinion column. I did not treat positive as a higher grade.",
    "Class_Attendance": "Question 22 is class attendance: always, sometimes or never. The synopsis talks about attendance, and this is the attendance column that actually exists in the UCI file. It is not a percentage. The internal-marks page on the website asks for an attendance percent separately, and that percent is not copied into this column and is not used to train the SVM.",
    "Midterm_Preparation_Company": "Question 23 asks with whom the student prepares for midterm exams: alone, with friends, or not applicable. Group study and studying alone are different habits. I kept both the company question and the timing question, because they are not the same fact.",
    "Midterm_Preparation_Timing": "Question 24 asks when the student prepares: close to the exam, regularly during the semester, or never. This matches the idea in the synopsis that preparation before the final result matters. The column is still a survey answer, not the internal mark itself.",
    "Taking_Notes": "Question 25 is taking notes in classes: never, sometimes or always. I kept it because it is a direct study habit. The exam module in the website also has a small question about notes, but that quiz question is stored in questions.csv. It is not this UCI column.",
    "Listening_in_Class": "Question 26 is listening in class: never, sometimes or always. It is close to attendance, but a student can attend and still not listen. I did not drop it as a duplicate. With 145 rows, two similar columns can still confuse a model, and that is one reason the accuracy stays low.",
    "Discussion": "Question 27 asks whether the student discusses the subject: never, sometimes or always. Discussion is a class activity. I kept the three answers as categories. There is no column for the marks given for participation.",
    "Flip_Classroom": "Question 28 asks whether a flip-classroom approach is useful, not useful, or not applicable. This is an opinion about a teaching method. It is not a mark. I kept it because the UCI authors included it in the questionnaire.",
    "Last_Semester_CGPA": "Question 29 is the last-semester CGPA band. I decoded it for the dictionary and then excluded it from the deployed model. Last-semester CGPA is already a result. If I train on it, the model is partly copying an old grade. Table 7.3 shows the small change when this column is added back. That run is only a check.",
    "Expected_Graduation_CGPA": "Question 30 is the CGPA the student expects at graduation. This is the student's guess about the future result. Using it as an input would mix the label with the student's own prediction. I excluded it. The data dictionary still lists every band so that a reader can see what was removed.",
}


def column_section() -> list[str]:
    blocks = [
        "H2 5.10 Notes on each questionnaire column",
        "In this section I have written what I understood from each question in the UCI file. The code list is also given in Appendix A. Here I have added why I kept the column or why I removed it. I have not copied the UCI web page line by line. These notes are my own reading of the file while doing the project.",
    ]
    for index, (name, note) in enumerate(COLUMN_NOTES.items(), start=1):
        blocks.append(f"H3 5.10.{index} {name.replace('_', ' ')}")
        blocks.append(note)
        blocks.append(
            f"In the program the column name is {name}. During training, each answer becomes its own yes or no column. "
            "If a new form sends an answer that was never seen in the 145 rows, one-hot encoding ignores that unknown answer instead of crashing. "
            "I checked this idea while reading the scikit-learn pipeline, and the tests check that the banned columns are absent from the feature list."
        )
    return blocks


CHAPTERS = {
    "1": """
H2 1.7 How this report follows the synopsis
The approved synopsis says the project should warn the teacher when a student may fall in Excellent, Average or Poor, using study information, and that the system should have admin, teacher and student work. It also describes exams, a question bank and internal marks. The UCI file I downloaded does not contain question papers or internal-mark columns. I did not type imaginary internal marks into the training file.

I divided the work into two parts that stay separate. The first part is the machine-learning model. It reads the 145-row UCI file and predicts a category from the questionnaire. The second part is the college workflow on the website. The teacher can store questions, the student can submit the short exam, and the teacher can enter internal marks, assignment marks and an attendance percent. The exam percentage uses its own rule: 75 and above is Excellent, 40 to 74 is Average, and below 40 is Poor. That rule is not the SVM result. The Predict page still shows the SVM category. A reader should not mix the two numbers.

H2 1.8 Users of the system
The admin looks after the teacher list and can open the evaluation table. The teacher looks after the question bank and the internal-mark register, and can also see the 145 UCI rows and the model table. The student fills the prediction form, attempts the exam, and reads his or her own attempt list. A student who opens the full student table is sent back to the dashboard. The message on that page says the records are limited to Admin and Teacher. I tested that path.

There is no separate account for every student in the UCI file. Those rows are historical survey rows, not logins. The three demo passwords are written on the login page so that the viva can be shown on one laptop. I have not built a forgot-password screen.

H2 1.9 What the project does not claim
I do not claim that the selected model is ready for a college result. Chapter VII shows that always answering Average is more accurate than the SVM on this feature set. I also do not claim that the short exam in the website was used to train the model. The exam file is created when the program starts, if it is not already present. The training file remains data/uci_student_performance.csv.

The project does not rank students against each other. The synopsis mentioned ranking in some places. I have a category, not a rank list, because a rank from a weak model would look more exact than the data allows.
""",
    "2": """
H2 2.8 How I read the papers
I did not reproduce the experiments from the papers. I read them to decide what to compare and what not to use as an input. Romero and Ventura describe prediction as one task inside educational data mining, along with clustering and relation mining [1]. I have done only prediction, and only with three classes. Baker and Yacef say that public datasets are widely used and that the field needs careful evaluation [2]. That is why I have reported macro-F1, accuracy and a majority baseline, not a single percentage.

Han, Kamber and Pei explain classification, training data and test data [3]. I used their idea that the class label is the output. Cortez and Silva compared several algorithms on two school subjects and discussed student background and study time [4]. My comparison also uses more than one algorithm. Their dataset is not my dataset, so I have not copied their accuracy into my tables.

Yilmaz and Sekeroglu studied the same 145-row collection and classified end-of-term performance [5]. The UCI page is the download I used [6]. Their paper can use a different feature list and a different split. I have not written their percentage as my result. My result is the number produced by my program and stored in results/metrics.json.

Quinlan's paper is the reference for building a decision tree from examples [7]. I used a shallow tree because 145 rows are easy to memorise. Breiman's random forest builds many trees and votes [8]. I used 300 trees. Cortes and Vapnik describe the support vector classifier [9]. Pedregosa and the other scikit-learn authors document the library I called from Python [10].

H2 2.9 Points I kept from the comparison
The papers do not agree on one best algorithm. I therefore trained five and selected with a rule I can explain in the viva. The papers also show that previous marks are strong inputs. I removed grade and both CGPA questions so that the model cannot copy a result it is supposed to predict. The remaining gap is that study-habit questions on this small file do not separate the three classes well. That gap is the result of the project, not a missing chapter.
""",
    "3": """
H2 3.8 Hardware table
The guideline asks for the machine on which the project can run. I have listed a minimum that is enough for this program. Training 145 rows does not need a graphics card.

H2 3.9 Who can do what
The admin can log in, open the dashboard, open prediction, open the student table, open the evaluation page, and add or remove a teacher name. The admin does not edit the UCI grades. The teacher can log in, manage questions, enter internal marks, see attempts through the files, and open the same evaluation page. The student can log in, submit the exam, read my-result, and submit the questionnaire form. The student cannot open the question-bank editor. If the student tries, the site sends him back.

Passwords in this demo are admin123, teacher123 and student123. They are short because the site is only on the local computer. I would not reuse them on a public server.

H2 3.10 Operating constraints
The site binds to 127.0.0.1 and port 8080. On my Mac, port 5000 is used by AirPlay. Opening that port shows a 403 page that is not my Flask program. I have written the correct address in Appendix B. The program trains once at startup. A second click on Predict does not run the five-fold comparison again. The tables in Chapter VII come from the training run that wrote metrics.json.

The question bank, internal marks and exam attempts are CSV files under the data folder. They can be deleted to reset the demo. Deleting the UCI file will stop training, and the program will not invent a replacement file.
""",
    "4": """
H2 4.10 How to read the diagrams
Fig. 4.1 shows the two paths. The top path is the UCI file, decoding, model comparison and the SVM used by the form. The side note says the exam CSV is not an input to training. Fig. 4.2 is the context data-flow diagram. Admin, teacher and student are outside the system. The UCI file, the question file and the marks file are the outside stores. Fig. 4.3 breaks the system into login, prediction, training, exam, internal marks and evaluation.

Fig. 4.4 is the use-case diagram. The ovals are the actions. Manage teachers is an admin action. The question bank and internal marks are teacher actions. Take exam is a student action. Predict category is available after login. Fig. 4.5 is the class diagram of the three Python parts I wrote: the Flask pages, the pipeline and the college record functions, with the question, internal mark and attempt data. Fig. 4.6 is the sequence for one exam submission. Fig. 4.7 is the activity from login to the role branch. Fig. 4.8 is the entity diagram. The UCI student row is not the same entity as the exam attempt.

H2 4.11 Design decisions
I kept one Flask process and no database server. A database would be more work than this dataset needs, and the university project can be shown from CSV files. I kept the label rule in one place in ml_pipeline.py so that the website and the report use the same Excellent, Average and Poor definition. The exam band is a second function in college_records.py. The two functions are not the same code path.

The prediction form is built from the feature column list. If I exclude a column in the pipeline, the form stops asking for it. Course id and sex are excluded, so the form does not ask for them. Grade is not on the form. The student cannot type the answer the model is supposed to guess.
""",
    "5": """
H2 5.11 Exam and internal marks in code
college_records.py creates four files if they are missing: questions.csv, internal_marks.csv, exam_attempts.csv and teachers.csv. The question file is seeded with eight short questions I wrote for the demo, on study habits, attendance, notes, assignments, internal assessment, revision, weekly planning and asking the teacher for help. They are not copied from a university question paper.

When the teacher saves an internal record, the program converts internal marks out of 30 into 50 percent of the score, assignment marks out of 20 into 30 percent, and attendance percent into 20 percent. The sum is then banded. A blank or a word in a numeric box shows an error on the page instead of saving a zero by accident only when the conversion fails. I still expect the teacher to type marks inside the stated limits. The page does not yet block a number above 30. That is a limit of this version.

When the student submits the exam, each question is compared with the stored answer letter. The score is the number of correct letters. The percent is score divided by the number of questions, multiplied by 100. The attempt is appended to exam_attempts.csv. The my-result page lists only the rows for the logged-in username.

H2 5.12 Training steps in plain language
The program reads the CSV with pandas. It maps every code through the dictionary in COLUMN_SPECS. It builds Performance_Category from the letter grade. It drops the excluded names. It builds five pipelines. Each pipeline imputes with the most frequent value, one-hot encodes, scales, and then calls the classifier. Cross-validation is stratified, so each fold keeps a mix of the three classes. The winning name is the highest mean macro-F1. The program then fits that winner on all 145 rows for the form. The metrics file is written before that final fit is used as the published score. The published score remains the cross-validation score.

The holdout split is 80 percent train and 20 percent test. 145 times 0.2 is 29, so the confusion matrix is on 29 students. 145 minus 29 is 116 training rows in that one split. I do not choose the winner from these 29 rows.
""",
    "6": """
H2 6.7 More checks I did by reading the pages
I logged in as admin and opened the teacher page. The default row is Class Teacher and General. Adding a name and a subject adds a row. Remove deletes that name. I logged in as teacher and opened the question bank. The eight seed questions are listed with the answer letter. Remove deletes one id. The add form needs a topic, a question, four options and a letter. I logged in as student and opened the exam. Every question has four radio buttons. Submit writes one attempt. My result shows the percent and the category. I then opened Predict and submitted a profile. The category on that page comes from the model, and it can disagree with the exam category. That disagreement is expected.

I also tried the student login on the question page and on the internal-marks page. Both redirect to the dashboard. The teacher login can open evaluation and the word Macro-F1 is on that page. Wrong password stays on the login page with the invalid-password line.

H2 6.8 Calculation check for the holdout
I used the confusion matrix in Chapter VII as a manual check. Correct predictions are the diagonal cells. Their sum divided by 29 is the holdout accuracy. It will not equal the five-fold accuracy, because the five-fold score averages five different test folds. If a cell in the Poor column and the Excellent row is non-zero, an Excellent student was called Poor. I have not edited the matrix to make the diagonal larger.

H2 6.9 Regression of the old pages
After I added the exam routes, I ran the old tests again. The student is still blocked from the student table. The teacher still sees the metric table. A prediction of the first data row still returns one of the three words. I did not delete those tests when I added the new pages.
""",
    "7": """
H2 7.8 Reading each model without hiding the weak score
Decision Tree is limited to depth 5. On this data its macro-F1 stays near the other models and its accuracy stays under the baseline. A deeper tree would fit the 145 rows more closely and would look better on the training rows, which is not the test I want.

Logistic Regression is the linear comparison. After one-hot encoding, many columns are 0 or 1 and some of them move together, because a question's answers are exclusive. The program can print a numeric warning for that model. I did not delete the model from the table because of the warning. Its score is finite and it is not the winner.

Random Forest has a higher accuracy than some of the others in the cross-validation table, but its Poor recall is weak and its macro-F1 is not the best. I did not select it on accuracy. Selecting on accuracy would favour the Average class.

Naive Bayes has its own assumptions. The one-hot columns of a single question are not independent, because exactly one of them is usually 1. A low score here is understandable. I still report it so that the comparison has the five names from the plan.

SVM is the selected model when its mean macro-F1 is the highest. The RBF kernel can draw a curved boundary. With 145 rows the boundary is not stable, which is why the fold standard deviation is reported beside the mean. Class weight balanced stops the model from ignoring the smaller classes completely, but the Poor recall is still low. I have not claimed that balanced weights solved the imbalance.

H2 7.9 What a teacher can do with this output
If the model says Poor, the teacher can look at attendance and study hours on the form and talk to the student. The teacher should not announce the label as a final mark. If the model says Excellent, the same caution applies, because many Excellent students fall in other cells of the confusion matrix. The internal-mark page is the place for the marks the teacher actually gave. The prediction page is the place for the questionnaire estimate. Keeping both pages is how I matched the synopsis without mixing the two kinds of data.
""",
    "8": """
H2 8.5 What I would say in the viva
I would start from the dataset size and the three class counts. I would then show Table 7.1 and say that SVM won on macro-F1 and still lost to the Average baseline on accuracy. I would open Table 7.3 and explain why course id was removed. I would log in as teacher, add nothing if the seed questions are enough, and then log in as student, submit the exam, and show that the exam category is a percentage band. I would open Predict and show a second category from the model. If the two categories differ, I would say that this is the correct behaviour of this project.

H2 8.7 Where the project stands
The comparison is finished and the website runs on this laptop. The selected model is recorded in the metrics file and repeated in Chapter VII. The exam and the internal marks are present as their own pages, and they are not training columns. The diagrams in Chapter IV match those pages: data flow, use case, class, sequence, activity and the entity diagram. The cover carries the semester, the programme, the roll number and the examination year. The declaration is a separate file. What remains outside this program is the department plagiarism check, my signature on that declaration, and the one-sided hard-bound copy sent to the department in Mysore. I have not written a plagiarism percentage into the declaration, because I have not received that percentage yet.

H2 8.6 Limits I will not hide
The training data is not from my college. The exam questions are short demo questions. The passwords are fixed. There is no digital signature on the declaration page inside the portal file, because that page has to be signed by me after I read the report and after the plagiarism tool used by the department has been run. This report does not contain a plagiarism percentage. The hard-bound copy is a printing step after the PDF is ready. It is not produced by the Python program.
""",
}


