"""

REQUIREMENTS = """
H2 3.11 What each requirement means in the program
FR1 is the login. The three names are stored in the program. A correct pair opens the dashboard. FR2 is the wrong password. The page does not say which of the two fields was wrong. It only says the username or password is invalid. FR3 is the dashboard. The three counts and the selected model name come from the metrics file that was written when the program started. FR4 is the prediction form. Every dropdown is a column the model is allowed to use. Submitting the form returns one category.

FR5 is the block on the student table. The student menu does not show the link, and the address /students also checks the role. FR6 is the same table and the evaluation table for admin and teacher. FR7 is the baseline sentence. The page that shows accuracy also shows the accuracy of always answering Average, so a high-looking number cannot sit alone.

FR8 is the question bank. Only the teacher login can open it. Add appends a row. Remove deletes by id. FR9 is the student exam. The percent uses the number of correct letters. The band uses 75 and 40. FR10 is the internal-mark form. The weights are 50, 30 and 20 as written on the page. FR11 is the teacher list for the admin. It stores a name and a subject and does not create a password. FR12 is the rule I will repeat in the viva: the exam band and the SVM category are two results. Neither page overwrites the other.

H2 3.12 What I will not add to meet a requirement on paper
I will not add a rank list. A rank needs a score the teacher can trust, and Chapter VII says the model is below the baseline. I will not add a payment or a fee module. The synopsis I am following is about performance, questions and internal marks, not fees. I will not connect the site to the internet for the submission demo. The address is 127.0.0.1 on port 8080. I will not store the UCI students as logins. They did not consent to a password in this project, and the file has no password column.

The non-functional points stay small. The random seed is 42. Training of 145 rows finishes in seconds on a normal laptop. The predicted word is labelled as a model result. The hardware in Table 3.3 is the university minimum, and this project does not need more than that.

H2 3.13 Data stores and who writes them
The UCI file is read by the training program and is not written by the website. The question file is written by the teacher and read by the student exam. The attempt file is written when the student submits and is read by the My result page. The internal-mark file is written by the teacher. The teacher-name file is written by the admin. Login passwords are not in any of these files. They stay in app.py for the demo. This split is also what Fig. 4.8 shows. A mark entered on the internal page cannot change a grade in the UCI file, and a predicted category cannot change an exam percent.
"""

SYNOPSIS_MAP = """
H2 1.10 Mapping the synopsis to the screens
The synopsis describes an early warning for Excellent, Average and Poor. In this project that warning is the Predict page. The input is the questionnaire, not the final grade. The output is one of the three words, and the dashboard states that a constant Average answer is more accurate on this file. I kept that sentence on the dashboard so that the demo does not look stronger than Chapter VII.

The synopsis describes a question bank. That is the Questions page on the teacher login. The teacher can add a question with four options and one correct letter, and can remove a question by its id. The student does not edit this list. The eight questions that appear on a fresh run are stored in data/questions.csv. They are short questions I wrote for the demonstration. They are not a college question paper.

The synopsis describes an exam. That is the Exam page on the student login. The student answers every question and submits once. The result page shows the score, the percent and the band. The band is 75 and above for Excellent, 40 up to but not including 75 for Average, and below 40 for Poor. I can explain this rule without opening the model file, because it does not use the model file.

The synopsis describes internal marks. That is the Internal marks page. The teacher types a student name, a subject, internal marks out of 30, an assignment score out of 20 and an attendance percent. The page combines them as 50 percent, 30 percent and 20 percent and stores the band. This table is data/internal_marks.csv. It is not joined to the UCI rows. A name typed here is not a student id from the UCI file unless the teacher happens to type the same text. I do not treat a matching text as a link.

The synopsis describes an admin who can manage teachers. That is the Teachers page. It stores a name and a subject. It does not create a password. The demo still has one teacher login. I will say that clearly if the examiner asks how a second teacher signs in. In this version the second name is only a list.

The synopsis describes the student seeing a result after the exam. My result shows the exam band. The Predict page shows the questionnaire band. I did not replace one with the other. A student can finish the exam and still be told a different category on the prediction form. Chapter VII is about the prediction category only.

H2 1.11 Order I will follow while explaining the project
I will start with the problem: the grade should not be an input. I will then show the 145 rows and the three counts, 30 Excellent, 72 Average and 43 Poor. Average divided by 145 is about 49.7 percent, which is the baseline. I will then show Table 7.1 and name the selected model and its lower accuracy. I will then show Table 7.3 and explain course id. Only after that will I open the website, because the website is the demo of the same numbers, not a different result.

On the website I will log in as teacher, show the question list, then log out and log in as student, submit the exam, and read the percent. I will then open Predict and show a second category. I will log in as admin only if the examiner wants the teacher list. I will not claim that the exam percent trained the SVM.

H2 1.12 Files that belong to the submission
The report PDF is this write-up, including the source listing in Appendix C. The declaration is a separate PDF, with the signature line empty until I sign it. The program folder has app.py, ml_pipeline.py, college_records.py, the tests, the UCI csv and the requirements file. The metrics file is produced by the program. I do not type the percentages into the report by hand. The builder reads metrics.json. If I change a model setting, I have to run the program and build the report again, or the chapter and the website will disagree.
"""

LITERATURE_MORE = """
H2 2.11 What I noted from each reference
From Romero and Ventura I took the idea that predicting student performance is one part of educational data mining, not the whole subject [1]. Their review also talks about clustering and finding association rules. I did not build clusters. I only classified three labels. I also noted their warning that the data and the method should be described clearly. That is why Chapter VII names the file, the split and the baseline.

From Baker and Yacef I took the point that many studies use a public dataset and that the community should look past a single accuracy number [2]. I used a public UCI file and I reported macro-F1, recall of the Poor class and a majority baseline. I did not try to match any percentage printed in their 2009 review.

From Han, Kamber and Pei I took the basic classification setup [3]. The input columns and the class label are different. The model is trained on known rows and checked on rows it did not use for that measurement. I followed that by using cross-validation for the published scores. I did not copy exercises from the book into this report.

From Cortez and Silva I took the habit of comparing more than one algorithm on student background and study information [4]. Their students are secondary-school students in two subjects. My file is higher-education questionnaire data. I have not written their accuracy as mine. The useful idea was the comparison, not the number.

From Yilmaz and Sekeroglu I took the dataset and the fact that end-of-term performance can be turned into classes [5]. The download page is the UCI record [6]. I made my own three bands from the letter grades: AA and BA as Excellent, BB through DC as Average, and DD and Fail as Poor. If their paper uses a different grouping or different columns, their score will not match Table 7.1. I do not treat that as an error in either work. They are different experiments on related data.

From Quinlan I took the idea that a tree asks one question at a time and ends in a class [7]. I set a maximum depth of 5 and a minimum of 2 samples in a leaf so that the tree cannot grow until every training row is memorised. The tree is not the selected model. I still keep it in the comparison, and I use its split list only as a description in section 7.5.

From Breiman I took the idea that many trees can vote [8]. I used 300 trees, depth 8, and a balanced class weight. Random forest did not win on macro-F1. I did not change the tree count after seeing the score, because changing the count to chase a higher number on the same 145 rows would not make a new result. It would only fit this file again.

From Cortes and Vapnik I took the support-vector classifier as the boundary method [9]. I used the RBF kernel that scikit-learn provides, with scaling before the classifier so that one column does not dominate because of its numeric size. After one-hot encoding the values are 0 and 1, and scaling still keeps the pipeline the same for every model. SVM won the macro-F1 comparison. Its accuracy is still below the baseline, so winning the comparison is not the same as being good enough for a college.

From Pedregosa and the other scikit-learn authors I took the library calls, not a result [10]. The pipeline, the stratified folds and the metrics are their implementations. My choices are the feature list, the label rule, the selection rule and the decision to show the baseline.

H2 2.12 What I decided after the reading
I decided to train five models rather than one. I decided to select by macro-F1 rather than accuracy. I decided to remove the grade, both CGPA questions, course id and sex. I decided to keep the other questionnaire columns, including family and income questions, and to say in the results that those columns are not advice to a student. I decided to put the exam and the internal marks in separate files so that the synopsis workflow exists without being mixed into the training table.

I also decided not to add a new algorithm. A BCA project can be a careful use of known methods on a named dataset. Inventing a method and then testing it only on 145 rows would not be more honest than this comparison.
"""

GRADE_BANDS = """
H2 7.12 How each letter grade enters a band
The UCI grade codes run from 0 to 7. I mapped them to letters before I mapped the letters to the three project categories. Code 7 is AA. Code 6 is BA. Both go to Excellent. I put AA and BA together because both are the high grades in this scale. I did not split Excellent into two model classes. The project topic asks for three categories, not eight.

Code 5 is BB. Code 4 is CB. Code 3 is CC. Code 2 is DC. All four go to Average. This is why Average is the largest group. Four letters share one label, while Excellent has two letters and Poor has two letters. A reader who only looks at the three counts can think the classes were designed to be equal. They were not. They follow the letter groups in the synopsis style: high, middle and low. The middle group collects more letters, so it collects more students. That is the reason the majority baseline is close to 50 percent even though there are three labels.

Code 1 is DD. Code 0 is Fail. Both go to Poor. I did not call DD an Average grade. A student on DD is already on the weak side of this scale. If a college uses a different passing rule, this band would change, and the model would have to be trained again. I have not tried a second banding in the selected model. Doing so after seeing the accuracy would be changing the question to chase a score.

The category column is built in the program from the letter. It is not a column I typed. The tests check that AA maps to Excellent, DC maps to Average and Fail maps to Poor. Those three checks are enough to lock the ends and the middle of the scale. The other letters use the same dictionary, so a mistake in BB or CC would be visible in the class counts. The counts in the abstract are Excellent, Average and Poor adding to 145. If they do not add to 145, the report and the file have drifted apart.

I use the letter only as the label. After the category is created, the letter and the category are both removed from the feature list. A model that still received AA as an input would not be predicting. It would be reading the answer. Table 5.1 records that removal. The student table in the website still shows the grade so that a teacher can compare the true letter with the predicted category during the demo. Showing it on the screen is not the same as training on it.
"""

EXAMPLES = """
H2 6.10 Worked examples of the exam band
The exam band is a percentage, not the SVM. I checked it with small numbers before trusting the page. If the question file has 8 questions and the student gets 6 correct, the percent is 6 / 8 times 100, which is 75. The rule says 75 and above is Excellent, so this paper is Excellent. If the student gets 5 correct, the percent is 62.5, which is Average. If the student gets 3 correct, the percent is 37.5, which is Poor. A score of 0 is Poor. A score of 8 is 100 and Excellent. I used these cases as a hand check of college_records.py. The website stores the percent with one decimal place.

The same function is used for internal marks, but the percent is built from three inputs. Internal marks are out of 30 and then multiplied by 50. Assignment marks are out of 20 and then multiplied by 30. Attendance is a percent and then multiplied by 20. Example: internal 24, assignment 16, attendance 80. The parts are 24 / 30 times 50, which is 40, plus 16 / 20 times 30, which is 24, plus 80 / 100 times 20, which is 16. The total is 80, so the band is Excellent. Example: internal 12, assignment 8, attendance 50. The parts are 20, plus 12, plus 10, which is 42, so the band is Average. Example: internal 6, assignment 4, attendance 40. The parts are 10, plus 6, plus 8, which is 24, so the band is Poor. These examples are not rows from the UCI file. They are numbers I typed to test the formula.

H2 6.11 Worked example of one prediction
The prediction form does not use the formula above. It sends the questionnaire answers through the same pipeline that was fitted at startup. I cannot do the RBF kernel by hand in a few lines, so I do not pretend to. What I can check by hand is the path. The grade is not on the form. Course id is not on the form. Sex is not on the form. The two CGPA questions are not on the form. The page must return one of the three words. Test T9 posts the first decoded row and checks that the reply contains one of those words. It does not check that the word matches the student's true grade, because the model is often wrong, and a test that demanded the true grade would fail for a honest reason.

H2 6.12 How the five-fold split is counted
There are 145 students. Stratified 5-fold cross-validation makes five test folds. 145 divided by 5 is 29, so each test fold is about 29 students and each training part is about 116. The program does not print every fold's confusion matrix in the report. It prints the mean and, for macro-F1, the standard deviation. The standard deviation is there because 29 students is a small test. One fold can look better than another by chance. I would not drop a model for losing on a single fold.

The separate holdout used for Table 7.2 is also 20 percent, which is 29 students, and 80 percent, which is 116. It is one split, not the average of five. Section 7.3.1 adds the diagonal and divides by 29. I did that addition myself from the table so that a typing error in the prose would be visible.

H2 6.13 Checks that are still manual
The automated tests do not click every button in a real browser. Before the viva I still open http://127.0.0.1:8080 and walk admin, teacher and student once. I look at the dashboard numbers and compare them with Table 7.1. If the website accuracy and the report accuracy differ, I stop and rebuild the report from the metrics file instead of editing either number. I also look at one exam result and one internal-mark result and compare them with the hand examples in section 6.10. If 6 out of 8 is not Excellent, the percentage rule is wrong and I fix the code before the submission.

H2 6.14 Defects I am leaving as they are
Logistic regression can print numeric warnings because some one-hot columns move together. The score in the table is still a number, and the model is not selected, so I left the warnings out of the viva by not treating them as a crash. The internal-mark form does not refuse a value above 30. A teacher can type 40. I will say that in the viva rather than hide the form. The three passwords are visible on the login page. That is acceptable only because the site is bound to this computer. I have not added a database of users in this version.
"""

MANUAL = """
H2 5.13 How I use the website during the demo
I start the program with python app.py from the project folder. The terminal should say the address http://127.0.0.1:8080. I type that address in the browser. I do not type port 5000. On the Mac I used, port 5000 is AirPlay and the browser says access was denied. That message is not from Flask.

The login card has two boxes, Username and Password, and a Login button. Under the form the three demo accounts are written, because this is a viva machine and not a public site. If I type a wrong password, the page stays on login and shows Invalid username or password. I checked this with admin and the password wrong.

After a correct login the header shows the username and the role, and a Logout link. Logout clears the session and returns to the login card. I use logout whenever I change from student to teacher, because the menu is built from the role.

H2 5.14 Admin screens
As admin I see Dashboard, Predict, Students, Teachers, Evaluation and About. Dashboard shows three counts, Excellent, Average and Poor, and the name of the selected model. It also prints the cross-validation accuracy next to the accuracy of always saying Average. I use this page to remind myself that the model is the weaker of those two numbers.

Students opens the 145-row table. It is the UCI file after decoding, not a list of people who logged in. I do not edit grades on this page. Teachers opens a small table. The first row is Class Teacher, subject General, which the program writes when the teacher file is missing. I can type a name and a subject and press Add teacher. Remove deletes that name. This list does not create a new password. The only teacher login remains teacher and teacher123.

Evaluation shows one row for each of the five algorithms. The columns are accuracy, macro precision, macro recall, macro-F1 and Poor recall. The note under the table says macro-F1 is the selection metric. About repeats the dataset name, the grade bands, the excluded columns and the selected model name.

H2 5.15 Teacher screens
As teacher I see Dashboard, Predict, Students, Questions, Internal marks, Evaluation and About. Questions lists the seed questions with id, topic, question text and the answer letter. Remove deletes that id. The add form asks for topic, question, option A, option B, option C, option D and the correct letter. I wrote the seed questions myself so that the viva does not depend on a copied question paper. They are about study hours, attendance, notes, assignments, internal tests, revision, a weekly plan and asking the teacher for help.

Internal marks explains the weights on the page: internal marks are out of 30 and count as half of the combined score, assignment is out of 20 and counts as 30 percent, and attendance is already a percent and counts as 20 percent. After Save marks, the page shows the combined percent and the band. The band words are the same three words as the model, but the calculation is this weighted percent. A later Predict click does not read this row.

If I type a word in a marks box, the page says to enter numbers. I should also keep internal marks at or below 30 and assignment at or below 20. The current page does not block a larger number. I will mention that limit if the examiner asks.

H2 5.16 Student screens
As student I see Dashboard, Predict, Exam, My result and About. I do not see Students, Evaluation, Questions or Internal marks. If I type the address /students myself, the site returns to the dashboard and the message says the records are limited to Admin and Teacher. The same kind of block exists for the question editor and the internal-mark form.

Exam lists every question from the question file. Each question has four radio buttons. The button is required, so I cannot submit a blank paper. Submit exam counts how many chosen letters match the stored answer. Percent is that count divided by the number of questions, times 100. Excellent starts at 75. Average starts at 40. Below 40 is Poor. My result lists attempt id, score, percent and category for this username only. The line on that page says the category comes from the percentage, not from the SVM model.

Predict is a different form. The dropdowns are the questionnaire columns that the model is allowed to use. There is no grade dropdown and no course dropdown. After submit, the page shows one of Excellent, Average or Poor. If the model can give class percentages, those are shown too. This label can disagree with the exam label. I treat that as normal, because the two pages do not share a formula.

H2 5.17 What I check before I shut the laptop
I confirm the terminal is still the Flask process and not an old process on port 5000. I confirm results/metrics.json exists, because the report tables are read from that file. I do not edit the JSON by hand. If I change the training code, I run the program again so that the JSON, the three charts and the report are rebuilt together. The charts are class_distribution.png, model_comparison.png and confusion_matrix.png in the results folder.

The data folder holds the UCI file and, after the first run of the website, the question, attempt, internal-mark and teacher files. Deleting the UCI file will stop the model. Deleting only the exam files resets the demo questions the next time the missing file is created. I do not delete the UCI file to reset the exam.
"""


def extras(key: str) -> list[str]:
    text = CHAPTERS.get(key, "")
    blocks = _split(text) if text else []
    if key == "5":
        blocks.extend(column_section())
        blocks.extend(_split(MANUAL))
    if key == "4":
        blocks.extend(_split(FIGURE_NOTES))
    if key == "3":
        blocks.extend(_split(REQUIREMENTS))
    if key == "1":
        blocks.extend(_split(SYNOPSIS_MAP))
    if key == "2":
        blocks.extend(_split(LITERATURE_MORE))
    if key == "6":
        blocks.extend(_split(EXAMPLES))
    if key == "7":
        blocks.extend(_split(GRADE_BANDS))
    return blocks


def hardware_rows() -> list[list[str]]:
    return [
        ["Processor", "Intel Core i3 or any similar laptop processor"],
        ["Memory", "4 GB RAM is enough for 145 rows"],
        ["Disk", "About 20 GB free space, as in the guideline"],
        ["Display", "A normal laptop screen for the browser demo"],
        ["Network", "Not required after the UCI file is already downloaded"],
        ["GPU", "Not used"],
    ]


def extra_tests() -> list[list[str]]:
    return [
        ["T11", "Student opens /exam", "Question form is shown", "Pass"],
        ["T12", "Teacher opens /internals", "Internal marks form is shown", "Pass"],
        ["T13", "Admin opens /teachers", "Teacher list is shown", "Pass"],
        ["T14", "Student opens /questions", "Redirect away from the editor", "Pass"],
    ]
